// NEGATIVE TEST -- a macro body may not contain a string literal (N3-12).
//
// THIS REFUSAL REPLACES A SILENT WRONG EXPANSION, which is the only reason it
// exists. A macro body is stored as `ast::Token`s, and `AstToken::Literal` is a
// `String` that carries no KIND, so the reverse conversion in src/macros/mod.rs
// guesses with `parse::<i64>()`. A string literal therefore came back as a
// `Token::String` whose CONTENT still had its quotes, `tokens_to_string` quoted
// it again, and the re-lex produced `""` `hi` `""`.
//
// Measured at e8eb1a9, compiling and running with no diagnostic at all:
//     macro s!() { "hi" }
//     fn main() { print(s!()); }
// printed an EMPTY LINE. The string the author wrote reached the output as
// nothing.
//
// The cost of the refusal is named rather than hidden: `println!(e)` takes a
// string in every use anybody would write, so this makes that builtin
// unusable. It already was -- measured at e8eb1a9, `println!("x")` failed with
// "expected ')', found identifier 'x'", which is the same defect one layer
// later and with no explanation attached.
macro shout!() { "HEY" }

fn main() {
    print(shout!());
}
